Q: Pimpl idiom without using dynamic memory allocation A: <p>This answer builds up on <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/4922328/5267751">Matthieu M.</a>'s answer to provide copy/move constructor/assignment operator, and wrap all of them into a safe templated struct.</p>
<h2>The struct</h2>
<pre><code>#include&lt;cstddef&gt;
#include&lt;utility&gt;
#include&lt;memory&gt;

template &lt;typename T, std::size_t align, std::size_t minSize, std::size_t maxSize = minSize&gt;
class MakeSizeAndAlignKnown { // kind of like optional
public:
    template&lt;typename... Args&gt;
    MakeSizeAndAlignKnown(Args&amp;&amp;... args) {
        new(data) T(std::forward&lt;Args&gt;(args)...);
    }

    MakeSizeAndAlignKnown(MakeSizeAndAlignKnown const&amp; other) {
        new(data) T(*other);
    }

    MakeSizeAndAlignKnown(MakeSizeAndAlignKnown&amp;&amp; other) {
        new(data) T(std::move(*other));
    }

    MakeSizeAndAlignKnown&amp; operator=(MakeSizeAndAlignKnown const&amp; other) {
        *this = *other;
        return *this;
    }

    MakeSizeAndAlignKnown&amp; operator=(MakeSizeAndAlignKnown&amp;&amp; other) {
        *this = std::move(*other);
        return *this;
    }

    ~MakeSizeAndAlignKnown() {
        static_assert(minSize &lt;= sizeof(T) &amp;&amp; sizeof(T) &lt;= maxSize &amp;&amp; align % alignof(T) == 0);
        (*this)-&gt;~T();
    }

    T&amp; operator*() {
        return *std::launder(reinterpret_cast&lt;T*&gt;(&amp;data));
    }

    T const&amp; operator*() const {
        return *std::launder(reinterpret_cast&lt;T const*&gt;(&amp;data));
    }

    T* operator-&gt;() { return &amp;**this; }
    T const* operator-&gt;() const { return &amp;**this; }

private:
    alignas(align) char data[maxSize];
};
</code></pre>
<p>Its interface is similar to <code>optional</code> where you use <code>*</code> or <code>-&gt;</code> to access the internal data, and <code>=</code> copies the data if present.</p>
<p>It has zero overhead in terms of memory, however it requires all structs that contain it, and defined when the inner struct is not a complete type, to have out-of-line constructor/destructor etc., which may incur function call overhead.</p>
<p>Note that the destructor will always be generated, which will <code>static_assert</code> that the provided size and alignment is correct. It suffices if the actual alignment is a divisor of the provided alignment, they need not be exactly equal.</p>
<h2>Example usage</h2>
<p>in <code>a.h</code>, put the folowing</p>
<pre><code>struct Inner;

struct Outer {
    int y;
    MakeSizeAndAlignKnown&lt;Inner, 4, 4&gt; inner;

    Outer();
    Outer(Outer const&amp; other);
    Outer(Outer&amp;&amp; other);
    Outer&amp; operator=(Outer const&amp; other);
    Outer&amp; operator=(Outer&amp;&amp; other);
    ~Outer();
};

struct InnerNoCopy;

struct OuterNoCopy {
    int y;
    MakeSizeAndAlignKnown&lt;InnerNoCopy, 8, 8&gt; inner;

    OuterNoCopy();
    OuterNoCopy(OuterNoCopy const&amp; other) = delete;
    OuterNoCopy(OuterNoCopy&amp;&amp; other);
    OuterNoCopy&amp; operator=(OuterNoCopy const&amp; other) = delete;
    OuterNoCopy&amp; operator=(OuterNoCopy&amp;&amp; other);
    ~OuterNoCopy();
};
</code></pre>
<p>You can define the two <code>Outer</code> structs above while having <code>Inner</code> and <code>InnerNoCopy</code> being incomplete. However, the destructor and copy/move constructor/assignment operators need to know how to destroy/copy/move construct/assign the <code>Inner</code>, so they must be moved out-of-line. <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/9954553/5267751"><code>unique_ptr</code> has the same problem</a>.</p>
<p>Apart from requiring a function call indirection, this also <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/64770166/5267751">makes designated initializer unusable</a>.</p>
<p>In <code>a.cpp</code> file, put</p>
<pre><code>struct Inner {
    int x;
};

Outer::Outer() = default;
Outer::Outer(Outer const&amp; other) = default;
Outer::Outer(Outer&amp;&amp; other) = default;
Outer&amp; Outer::operator=(Outer const&amp; other) = default;
Outer&amp; Outer::operator=(Outer&amp;&amp; other) = default;
Outer::~Outer() = default;

struct InnerNoCopy {
    std::unique_ptr&lt;int&gt; x;
};

OuterNoCopy::OuterNoCopy() = default;
OuterNoCopy::OuterNoCopy(OuterNoCopy&amp;&amp; other) = default;
OuterNoCopy&amp; OuterNoCopy::operator=(OuterNoCopy&amp;&amp; other) = default;
OuterNoCopy::~OuterNoCopy() = default;
</code></pre>
<p>After the <code>struct Inner</code> are defined, the member functions can now be properly defined. <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/14256726/5267751">Usually you can just <code>= default</code> them</a>.</p>
<p>They can be used as follows.</p>
<p>In <code>b.cpp</code>:</p>
<pre><code>void f(){
    Outer outer;
    Outer outer2 = outer;
    outer = outer2;
    OuterNoCopy outer3;
    OuterNoCopy outer4 = std::move(outer3);
    outer3 = std::move(outer4);
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can still copy/move/destroy the objects.</p>
<p>In <code>a.cpp</code>, after the definition of <code>Inner</code>, you can do</p>
<pre><code>void g(){
    Outer outer;
    OuterNoCopy outer2;
    int x = outer.inner-&gt;x;
    int y = *outer2.inner-&gt;x;
}
</code></pre>
<p>i.e. access the members inside the <code>inner</code> members.</p>
